speechtext:
Paragraph (b) reads:
(b) respecting the packaging and labelling of any article of food or drug and the design of any such package or label with a view to preventing the public or the purchaser being deceived or misled as to the character, strength, quality or quantity of the article and requiring, notwithstanding anything contained in paragraph (f) of section seven of this act, a declaration of net contents upon any package containing a cosmetic;
I find myself in accord with the section, although I repeat that I should like to have the minister consider the insertion of words which would prevent exploitation in respect of price.

With all due respect to the minister and to the hon. member who has

just spoken, I think I may be allowed to say that I know as much about cosmetics as any man here. I use lipstick when I like it;
I do not care how much it weighs, or how little. The hon. member has spoken of flappers and of young people. The girls of to-day, and the same applied even to those of my day, from the time they are old enough to know anything about titivating, want a little powder. In my day we used rice powder, or even com starch. To-day the girls want a pretty compact; and if I like a compact I do not think about what I am going to get inside of it. Let me give hon. members a practical demonstration. I open my bag, I take out my compact, and I use it as much for the show and the beauty of it as for what it contains. While unfortunately I am not a flapper, I believe the minister can protect us by seeing that we do not use poison,-
